    "findings": "Baseline Superiority Findings: 1. Classification Accuracy: Model B achieves 50.0% Top-1 accuracy (12/24) vs 45.8% (11/24) for both grid baselines, demonstrating a +4.2% hit rate advantage (1 extra race correctly predicted). 2. Top-3 Coverage: The Empirical Grid Baseline achieves superior Top-3 coverage (83.3%, 20/24) compared to Model B (79.2%, 19/24) because in F1, starting in the top 3 on the grid has an overwhelming empirical correlation with podium conversion. 3. Ranking Capability (MRR): Model B achieves higher Mean Reciprocal Rank (0.697) than both the Empirical Grid Baseline (0.686) and Reciprocal Baseline (0.654). 4. Probability Loss Tradeoff: When in-sample calibrated, Model B has higher log loss (2.283 vs 1.853) than the empirical grid baseline because the empirical grid baseline produces conservative, well-spread probabilities (e.g. pole ~45%, P2 ~20%, P3 ~12%), whereas LightGBM produces sharper logits that suffer larger NLL penalties when a non-pole driver wins."
